# Membral cloud environment (apps/cloud) — see docs/DEPLOY.md for the full
# hosted-deployment walkthrough (issue #12), or README.md#run-it-locally for a
# local-only run. Vars are grouped REQUIRED (the app fails fast at boot in
# production if any is missing — see src/lib/env.ts's validateEnvAtBoot /
# instrumentation.ts) and OPTIONAL (a documented degraded behaviour when
# absent, never a crash).
#
# ⚠️ PRODUCT CONSTRAINTS (issue #127 self-hosting decision, 2026-09-08):
# most of what gates HOW MUCH an account can spend (llm/embeddings on/off
# and budgeted, new-signup conversation_limit) is NOT an env var at all —
# it's a single DB-level switch, `app_settings.mode` (migration 081), read
# by scripts/flags.ts mode show|open|hosted. NO ROW = 'open' — every var
# fresh from THIS file (a fresh clone/local dev/CI) runs with NO product
# constraints until you explicitly set `mode hosted`. Run
# `npm run flags --workspace=@membral/cloud -- mode show` for the current mode, and
# docs/DEPLOY.md step 1a for the one command a HOSTED deployment must run
# right after `db push` to avoid running unconstrained against a real
# OPENAI_API_KEY. The env vars below (MEMBRAL_LLM_DEFAULT_BUDGET_USD,
# MEMBRAL_EMBED_DEFAULT_*, MEMBRAL_FEATURE_ALLOWLIST) only take effect in
# `hosted` mode — see the "Feature budgets & teaser" section below.

# ============================== REQUIRED ===================================
# All four below are required in production. Locally, only the three
# Supabase vars matter day-to-day (getEnv() in lib/env.ts still fail-fasts on
# them the moment a Supabase-backed route runs); NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL falls
# back to http://localhost:3000 outside production so local dev needs no
# extra setup.

# Printed by `npx supabase start` (local) or Project Settings -> API (hosted).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
# Server-only — never expose to the client. Bypasses RLS; used for
# service-role reads/writes (webhooks, background pipeline, health check).
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# The app's own canonical public URL. Replaces every hard-coded
# `localhost:3000` fallback (auth redirects, OG/metadata, MCP config JSON) via getAppUrl() in lib/env.ts. Local default:
# http://localhost:3000 (no need to set this var for local dev). Production:
# https://membral.me — the one public domain this product targets.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=

# ============================== OPTIONAL ====================================

# --- LLM pipeline ---
# OPENAI_API_KEY unset (and MEMBRAL_MODEL_BASE_URL also unset) => LLM-backed
# features (memory extraction, embeddings) are simply unusable
# until set; nothing crashes, requests to those code paths fail gracefully.
OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
# Chat model. Default gpt-4o-mini (the honest quality floor). Ollama 13B+ is the
# advanced opt-in floor; sub-7B is NOT recommended (see ADR 0001 Finding 5).
MEMBRAL_MODEL=
# OpenAI-compatible base URL for a local model (e.g. http://localhost:11434/v1
# for Ollama). Leave blank to use OpenAI.
MEMBRAL_MODEL_BASE_URL=
# Embedding-specific base URL override (falls back to MEMBRAL_MODEL_BASE_URL,
# then api.openai.com). Rarely needed — only when embeddings must come from a
# different endpoint than chat completions.
MEMBRAL_EMBEDDINGS_BASE_URL=
# Embedding model (MUST be 1536-dim to match the schema). Default
# text-embedding-3-small. MEMBRAL_EMBED_MODEL is the older name, still read
# as a fallback if this is unset.
MEMBRAL_EMBEDDING_MODEL=
# Per-run cost cap in USD (issue #21) — a bootstrap/sweep run halts mid-run once
# LlmClient.totalCostUsd reaches this, leaving remaining work pending/resumable.
# Default 5 (ADR 0027) when unset/blank. `0` is a VALID, enforcing value — it
# halts at the very first budget check, before a single call is made. A
# non-blank value that fails to parse or is negative is logged as a warning and
# falls back to the default. NOTE (overshoot bound): a concurrency > 1 worker
# pool may have already claimed up to (concurrency - 1) other in-flight calls
# before the cap trips, so actual spend can overshoot the cap by up to
# (concurrency - 1) x the most expensive single call — the cap is a backstop,
# not a hard ceiling on any single tick.
MEMBRAL_KG_COST_CAP_USD=
# Rolling 30-day per-user spend ceiling in USD (ADR 0027 J6.2) — refuses to START
# a new run when trailing-30-day spend (llm_spend ledger) already meets/exceeds
# this. Set an explicit dollar figure before your first real user (ADR 0027
# proposed $2/user/month as the reference point; never rely on the fallback
# below as your actual budget decision). `0` is a VALID, enforcing value — it
# blocks every run outright (spend can never be negative). A non-blank value
# that fails to parse or is negative is logged as a warning and falls back to
# "not enforcing" (null), same as blank — EXCEPT in production (NODE_ENV=
# production), where leaving this blank/unparseable now applies a conservative
# built-in default of $25/user/30-day (logged once as a warning) instead of not
# enforcing at all — see checkMonthlyCeiling in lib/kg/spend-ledger.ts. Local
# dev and test are unaffected (NODE_ENV isn't "production" there) and keep the
# old "not enforcing" behaviour when this is blank. NOTE: this ceiling is
# NOT affected by `open`/`hosted` app mode (issue #127 self-hosting
# decision) — it protects YOUR OWN OPENAI_API_KEY from runaway spend, not a
# per-account product limit, so it applies in production regardless of
# mode. If you self-host with `next start` in production, this default
# still applies to you.
MEMBRAL_KG_MONTHLY_CEILING_USD=
# Bootstrap-only fallback gating LLM-backed features per-user BEFORE
# `apps/cloud/scripts/flags.ts` has ever been run against this project (issue
# #126, ADR 0047) — NOT a standing config var; remove it once you've granted
# your own `feature_flags` row (see docs/DEPLOY.md §2a). The `feature_flags`
# table is the real, permanent gate: an explicit row (enabled=true OR
# enabled=false) always wins over this var, so removing an email/id from this
# list does NOT turn anything off once a row exists — see hasFeature in
# lib/feature-flags.ts. Format: `feature:entry1,entry2;feature2:entry3`
# (semicolon-separated feature groups, each a comma-separated entry list),
# entries either an email or a Supabase auth user id (a uuid — the `id`
# column of `auth.users`), e.g. "llm:you@example.com,<your-user-id>".
# Case-insensitive on the feature key and on emails. An email entry only matches when that
# account's email is CONFIRMED (auth.users.email_confirmed_at set) — prefer a
# user-id entry, which has no such wait and can't be squatted via signup.
MEMBRAL_FEATURE_ALLOWLIST=

# --- Feature budgets & teaser (issue #128, ADR 0047 v2) ---
# Defaults applied ONLY when a user has no explicit `feature_flags` row,
# is not on MEMBRAL_FEATURE_ALLOWLIST above, AND the instance is in
# `hosted` app mode (issue #127 self-hosting decision, migration 081 —
# `npx tsx scripts/flags.ts mode show`). In `open` mode (the default with
# no `app_settings` row — see the constraints callout at the top of this
# file) both features are enabled and UNLIMITED with no budget at all, and
# every var below is simply unused. See checkFeature in
# lib/feature-flags.ts. `embeddings` is on for every fresh HOSTED-mode
# signup, bounded by a lifetime budget then a small daily trickle once
# that's used up.
# Lifetime USD budget for the "embeddings" feature. Default 0.50 if unset.
MEMBRAL_EMBED_DEFAULT_BUDGET_USD=0.50
# Trailing-24h USD trickle for "embeddings" once the lifetime budget above is
# exhausted. Default 0.01 if unset.
MEMBRAL_EMBED_DEFAULT_DAILY_USD=0.01
# Lifetime USD budget for the "llm" feature (KG/portraits/bridge, and memory
# v1 source C's tail capture) applied to every account with no explicit
# grant. Maintainer decision 2026-09-13 (issue #151): the code default when
# this is UNSET is 0 — no LLM allowance until a pricing review. Set this to
# grant every hosted signup a default allowance, or grant a specific amount
# per-user instead:
#   npx tsx scripts/flags.ts grant --feature llm --email x@y.com --budget-usd 0.15
# (a "teaser" grant — roughly the cost of the latest ~20 chats' pass-1
# extraction, ~$0.0068/conversation measured on the founder corpus). Omit
# --budget-usd entirely for a full, unlimited enable.
MEMBRAL_LLM_DEFAULT_BUDGET_USD=0
# Round-5 verification F6 — per-user spend RATE on hosted, not just lifetime
# budget. Applied ONLY in `hosted` app mode (issue #127), and ONLY to a
# "full" llm grant — an explicit `feature_flags` row (from `scripts/flags.ts
# grant --feature llm`, with no --budget-usd/--daily-usd of its own) that
# would otherwise be genuinely unlimited. Default 2 (USD/day) if unset —
# bounds a granted account to that much per day once granted, so one account
# can't become an unbounded spend source against your key. Does NOT apply:
# in `open` mode (no product constraints, issue #127); to a grant that
# already has its own --budget-usd or --daily-usd; or to a grant made with
# `--daily-usd none` (an explicit, permanent unlimited override — see
# `scripts/flags.ts grant`'s own help text).
MEMBRAL_LLM_DEFAULT_DAILY_USD=2

# --- LLM spend caps: per user vs global (issue #335) ---
# All three are USD, summed from the llm_spend ledger. Blank = the default;
# an invalid or negative value = 0, which blocks every LLM call it covers.
#
# PER USER: each account's memory extraction + pattern drafts spend, per UTC
# calendar month, in `hosted` mode. NOT a total — the worst case is this
# value times the number of accounts. Default 2.
MEMBRAL_MEMORY_MONTHLY_BUDGET_USD=2
# GLOBAL: all accounts summed, per UTC day (since midnight UTC). Checked
# before every LLM call path (memory extraction, pattern drafts, every
# createCostGuardedLlm client, code-session summaries/roll-ups/cross-links).
# Default 2.
MEMBRAL_LLM_GLOBAL_DAILY_CEILING_USD=2
# GLOBAL: all accounts summed, per UTC calendar month (since the 1st). Same
# call paths as the daily ceiling. Default 30 (15 days of the daily default,
# so a run that pins the daily ceiling stops mid-month). If the sum can't be
# read, both global ceilings refuse the call (fail closed).
MEMBRAL_LLM_GLOBAL_MONTHLY_CEILING_USD=30

# Support address shown in every "not enabled"/"allowance used up" message
# (settings page, memory empty states, the API's own 403 body). Falls back to
# support@membral.me (the address already on /privacy) if unset.
MEMBRAL_CONTACT_EMAIL=support@membral.me

# --- Daily extraction cron (issue #132, ADR 0049) ---
# Shared secret GET /api/internal/cron/advance checks its Authorization
# header against. Required for the daily extraction cron on Vercel — Vercel
# sends it automatically as `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET` on every
# cron-triggered request once this var is set in the project's environment
# (https://vercel.com/docs/cron-jobs/manage-cron-jobs#securing-cron-jobs); no
# code change needed on your side to wire that up. Left unset, the route
# degrades SAFELY rather than crashing or running open: every request
# (cron-triggered or not) gets a 503, so "the daily fan-out simply doesn't
# run" is the failure mode, not an unauthenticated endpoint. This is a real
# gap worth noticing, not just a decoration: the cron is the safety net for
# a granted user who stops syncing (their extraction otherwise only advances
# on sync/import/self-advance); leaving this unset silently removes that net
# with no error anywhere an operator would see it short of noticing the cron
# always logs 503 in the Vercel function logs.
CRON_SECRET=

# --- Signups (issue #127) ---
# Emergency kill-switch: "1" or "true" refuses every new signup attempt
# app-level (the signup page shows "Signups are currently closed.") without
# touching Supabase Auth's own provider settings. Unset/blank => signups
# stay open (default). Toggle this first if abuse shows up on a public
# deployment; per-IP throttling (RATE_LIMIT_SIGNUP_IP*, see lib/rate-limit.ts)
# already applies unconditionally and needs no env var to be active.
#
# Round-2 review HIGH 5: this env var ONLY gates the Next.js signup form —
# Supabase's own GoTrue endpoint (POST /auth/v1/signup) is a separate origin,
# reachable directly with the public anon key, and never touches this check.
# The AUTHORITATIVE switch for a hosted deployment is Supabase Dashboard ->
# Authentication -> Providers -> Email -> "Allow new users to sign up" = off
# — flip that first in a real incident. `npx tsx scripts/flags.ts signups
# on|off|status` (migration 079's app_settings table + a before-insert
# trigger on auth.users) is a DB-level belt-and-braces layer that blocks a
# direct GoTrue call too, for when the dashboard toggle wasn't (or, in local
# dev, can't be) flipped in time — see docs/DEPLOY.md S6.
MEMBRAL_SIGNUPS_DISABLED=

# --- MCP ---
# Overrides the MCP server URL shown in Settings -> MCP config snippets
# (components/mcp-config.tsx). Unset => defaults to the browser's own
# origin + /api/mcp (correct for both local dev and a normal hosted
# deployment) — only set this if MCP is served from a different host than
# the main app.
NEXT_PUBLIC_MCP_URL=
# Set to 1 to also register the extended MCP tools (get_code_project,
# get_code_context_for, membral_correct). Off by default: agents see 9 tools, not 12.
MEMBRAL_MCP_EXTENDED_TOOLS=

# --- Debugging ---
# Comma-separated user UUIDs (no wildcard). For those users only, logs the FULL
# text of a model reply cut off at the output limit (memory extraction, issue
# #339). WARNING: that text can quote the user's transcript; use only on the
# maintainer's own account while debugging, and unset it afterwards.
MEMBRAL_DEBUG_CUTOFF_REPLY_USER_IDS=
